C and C++ projects use ordinary CMake builds with explicit source lists. The tables below describe Rivet's CMake integration; other languages use their provider's native tasks. For a first build, follow the [C++](/docs/first-project/) or [C](/docs/first-c-project/) tutorial.

## Know which files to edit

| File | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `.rivet.json` | Project choices, tracked files, targets, modules, and dependencies |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | Project entry point and your extensions |
| `cmake/RivetTargets.cmake` | Generated target definitions, sources, and linkage |
| `cmake/Dependencies.cmake` | Generated CPM declarations (C++ only) |
| `cmake/Options.cmake` | Editable warning/sanitizer options (C++ only) |
| `CMakePresets.json` | Shared configure, build, and test presets |
| `CMakeUserPresets.json` | Ignored machine-local compiler and cache choices |

File and target operations regenerate `RivetTargets.cmake`; dependency operations also update `Dependencies.cmake`. Rivet refuses to replace hand-edited generated content. Custom CMake belongs after the generated includes in `CMakeLists.txt` or in your own included file. See [Project ownership](/docs/project-model/) for the distinction.

Saving a new source file alone does not register it. [File templates](/docs/templates/) update registration as part of creation.

## Task sequences

| Task | Steps |
| --- | --- |
| Configure | Configure |
| Build | Configure, then Build |
| Test | Configure, Build, then CTest |
| Run | Configure, Build the selected application, then execute it |
| Format | Configure, then the `format` target |

A failed step stops the sequence. The generated `format` target requires `clang-format` to be found at Configure time. Source buffers must be saved before a build.

```sh
rivet build --preset debug
rivet test --preset debug
rivet run --preset release --target viewer
```

`viewer` must be an existing executable target. Shared presets use `build/debug` and `build/release`; Rivet prefers their `local-debug`/`local-release` overrides when available.

Direct CMake commands work too. For a generated local compiler preset:

```sh
cmake --preset local-debug
cmake --build --preset debug
ctest --preset debug
```

Without a local override, configure with `debug`. Generated local compiler presets keep the shared build directory. C++ projects also include optional Just recipes for configure, build, test, format, and application run.

## Presets and target selection

The editor remembers choices per project under `stdpath('state')/rivet/tasks/`:

| Command | Selection |
| --- | --- |
| `:RivetPreset` | Configure preset |
| `:RivetBuildPreset` | Associated build preset |
| `:RivetTestPreset` | Associated test preset |
| `:RivetBuildTarget` | Build target override |
| `:RivetRunTarget` | Runnable application |
| `:RivetTask repeat` | Last task, using the current selections |
| `:RivetStatus` | Display active choices |

A unique associated build/test preset is automatic; several require a choice. With none, Rivet uses the configured directory and configure environment. Build respects the preset's targets unless overridden. Run builds its selected application; Test follows build/test presets independently of the Build target override. Changing the configure preset clears dependent build/test selections.

Hidden or unavailable presets are excluded. CLI selection uses explicit flags. Build directories must be explicitly set inside the project, outside `.git`, and cannot traverse symlinks. In-source and external build directories are rejected. Running from a multi-config build requires a build preset specifying its configuration.

## Custom preset example

This C++ example enables address and undefined-behavior sanitizers with a compatible Clang/GCC compiler. Merge the arrays into `CMakeUserPresets.json`, keeping existing entries:

```json
{
  "version": 6,
  "configurePresets": [{
    "name": "asan",
    "inherits": "debug",
    "binaryDir": "${sourceDir}/build/asan",
    "cacheVariables": {
      "CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER": "/path/to/clang++",
      "RIVET_SANITIZERS": true
    }
  }],
  "buildPresets": [
    { "name": "asan-build", "configurePreset": "asan", "jobs": 4 }
  ],
  "testPresets": [{
    "name": "asan-test",
    "configurePreset": "asan",
    "output": { "outputOnFailure": true }
  }]
}
```

```sh
rivet test --preset asan --build-preset asan-build --test-preset asan-test
```

Rivet invokes native presets so their build targets, test filters, environments, and configuration choices apply. Local cache overrides created by Rivet retain the original build directory and preset associations.

## Compilation database and executable discovery

After Configure, Rivet copies an available compilation database to `.cache/rivet/compile_commands.json`. The generated `.clangd` points there. An unchanged legacy `.clangd` using only `build/debug` is migrated; custom configurations are preserved. Set their `CompilationDatabase` to `.cache/rivet` to follow the active configuration.

Executable paths come from CMake's File API, including `OUTPUT_NAME` and `RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY`. Module-aware editing also needs a successful build to produce module artifacts.

## Output and diagnostics

Editor tasks stream output. The window follows new lines while the cursor is at the end; scrolling up pauses that behavior. Ctrl-C stops the active task and remaining sequence; `q` closes the output window while the task continues.

Recognized GCC/Clang file/line diagnostics enter a workflow-specific quickfix list. `:copen`, `:cnext`, and `:cprevious` navigate them. Rivet keeps updating its own list if another list is selected; `:colder` and `:cnewer` switch lists. Messages without recognized source locations remain in task output.

## Cache editor

After Configure, `:RivetCMake` displays editable cache entries, types, and help text. Changes write a typed local user-preset override and reconfigure. CLI equivalents are:

```sh
rivet cache --preset debug
rivet cache RIVET_SANITIZERS ON --preset debug
```

`BUILD_TESTING` applies to both providers; `RIVET_WARNINGS` and `RIVET_SANITIZERS` are C++ starter options. The cache editor protects selected build/toolchain keys and CPM/FetchContent settings. Compiler, generator, and toolchain changes should be made explicitly in presets with a fresh compatible build directory.

## Doctor and tests

`:RivetDoctor` or `rivet doctor --preset asan` reports toolchain information without configuring the project. For C++, it checks standard-mode acceptance, module-tool information, and compilation-database freshness. A configured toolchain-file build is needed to identify its actual compiler. A syntax probe does not establish full language, library, or module support; see [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/).

CTest runs the dependency-free starter test: C++ checks work partition boundaries, while C checks addition. Additional frameworks use explicit [dependencies](/docs/dependencies/). [Parallel](/docs/hpc/) and [graphics](/docs/graphics/) profiles add separate runtime tests.
